<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Court (Bulgaria)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1944–1945</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-court-germany">People's Court</a> was a special tribunal set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> after the communist-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-bulgaria">Fatherland Front</a> took power in September 1944. In trials from December 1944 to 1945 it judged former regents, ministers, members of parliament, officers and others for collaboration and war crimes, passing more than 2,700 death sentences, among them those of Prince Kiril and three former prime ministers. It destroyed much of the country's old political elite.</p></div>
